!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/37/io/ltr8/cli/diagnostics.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/policy.tn"
@doc:"""
  The tson CLI's report: what a run found, and the policy it judged under. A
  project-owned schema (CLAUDE.md's "Project-owned schema !!id"): /2026/37 is
  the spec revision it is written against, io/ltr8 the publisher, cli this
  module's schema group.

  The policy a report states is the spec's own `policy` (policy.tn), imported
  rather than restated, so this report and any other processor's state one
  shape. What is this library's own is the rest: diagnostic_code and outcome
  are hand-written copies of Diagnostic.Code and Outcome, and
  DiagnosticsSchemaTest checks both against the Java enum in both directions,
  which is what catches a member added to one and forgotten here
  (PolicySchemaTest does the same for policy.tn's restriction_level).

  This enum copies Diagnostic.Code whole, not the subset one consumer happens to emit -- the same
  reason DUPLICATE_FIELD's absence was a defect rather than an economy.
  """
{
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED FIELD_GROUP TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF SCOPE_NOT_ADMITTED
    ATOM_FORM_INVALID ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION
    UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD CONFUSABLE_NAMES RESTRICTED_CHARACTER RESTRICTED_SCRIPT
    SCHEMA_ERROR UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR NOT_IMPLEMENTED BIND_MISMATCH LIMIT_EXCEEDED
    SCHEMA_NOT_PERMITTED SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND SCHEMA_UNREACHABLE SCHEMA_TIMEOUT SCHEMA_TOO_LARGE
  ]
  @doc:"""
    Whether the document will be accepted by the processor that judged it, under its schema, policy
    and limits.

    ACCEPTED: nothing was reported. REJECTED: the document is invalid, or this processor refused it
    under its own configuration -- its [TSON-DATA] §8.2 policy, its §9.1 limits, or a schema it does
    not hold and will not fetch; either way it will not be accepted here, and one rejection settles it
    whatever else went unjudged. Which kind each problem is, the portable finding or the local refusal,
    is its diagnostic_code. UNDETERMINED: nothing rejected the document, but something could not be
    judged -- a schema that could not be obtained, a type with no Java class here, a construct not
    implemented -- so acceptance cannot be stated.
    """
  outcome => !enum [ ACCEPTED REJECTED UNDETERMINED ]
  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be missing -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    Nothing to say is written as nothing: every field that may have nothing to say is optional here and
    is left out, in --output json as in --output tson. That includes both RFC 6901 pointers, and the
    distinction they draw is load-bearing: a present "" is the root, which a document-level schema
    problem genuinely carries, while a missing pointer means this diagnostic has no such end at all.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1).

    A §8.2 name-hygiene refusal is one of these like any other problem, told apart by its code --
    CONFUSABLE_NAMES for two names in one scope that read alike, RESTRICTED_CHARACTER for a character
    outside the identifier profile, RESTRICTED_SCRIPT for a script the restriction level does not admit
    (usually a combination, but at the ASCII-only level a single script with nothing mixed, which is why
    the code names what was refused rather than a mix). It carries no field of its own: what §8.2 requires
    a refusal to name -- the Unicode data version it was computed against -- is a fact about the
    processor rather than about the problem, constant for every refusal in the run, so the envelope
    states it once in `policy` and this states the remedy.
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path?: text?
    schema_pointer?: text?
    schema_id?: text?
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected?: text?
    actual?: text?
    data_position?: text?
    schema_position?: text?
  }
  validation_report => { outcome: outcome  policy: policy  errors: [diagnostic] }
  file_report => { file: text  outcome: outcome  errors: [diagnostic] }
  validation_run => { outcome: outcome  policy: policy  files: [file_report]  errors: [diagnostic] }
}
